WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.200
Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.560
My name is Alex Farach.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.340
Welcome to

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.990
Let's start at the beginning -
bits the character encoding

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.900
and.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.140
I'm a data scientist
and analytics manager

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.770
at Accenture Federal Services.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.820
I spend a lot of time thinking
about R, NLP, data viz,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.530
and statistical learning.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.410
My most recent work is on
the Hugging Face R package

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.210
to bring Hugging Face
models and tools to R.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.540
So how would you describe the
letter A to a computer? Today,

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.360
I'll discuss how.
In doing so, we'll

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.640
get into the shallow
end of a deep pool known

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.610
as bits to character
encoding, and we'll

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.130
talk a little bit of what
that looks like in R. So

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.640
what do we mean by bits

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.070
to character
encoding, especially

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.440
in relationship to
NLP? Well, computers

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.340
don't speak the same
language as we do.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.400
If we want to use computers
to process language,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.160
you need some way to encode
language so that computers

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.940
understand and vice versa.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.510
Both the encoding and
the decoding process

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.280
needs to be identical
on both ends

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.100
or the human computer
translation falls apart.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.730
The agreed upon method
is called a protocol.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.250
So back to the question how
would you describe the letter A

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.212
to a computer?

00:01:06.212 --> 00:01:07.920
Well, we do so by using
numbers, of course,

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:08.860
to represent letters.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.730
So instead of A we can have
1, V it could be 2, and so on.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.200
We assign a number to
represent each unique character

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:15.700
or symbol

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.990
we want to communicate. Computers
store numbers in binary,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.510
obviously, and all
signals inside a computer

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.100
have two possible
values, 0 or 1.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.250
Each of the 0/1 pieces of
information are called bits.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.080
One bit can represent two value.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.780
Two bits can represent
four, three bits

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.430
represent eight, and so on.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:36.040
So how many bits is needed
to represent 256 values?

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.020
Using the logic, just discussed,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.810
it's 8 bits.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.540
When computers were
first developed,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:44.040
the decision was made that a
byte was the next standard unit

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.000
above a bit.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.960
A byte is defined as 8 bits.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.990
In the 1960s,
computer developers

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.720
had one byte to work with.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.360
They developed a protocol
to fit into this one byte

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.680
called ASCII.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.930
This protocol can
assign a unique number

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.700
to all letters in
the English language,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.490
plus numbers, symbols
and control characters.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.440
Everything fits into
this one-byte budget.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.039
And even better, seven
of the available 8 bits

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.681
were needed. Over time,

00:02:09.681 --> 00:02:10.389
the need to fit

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:12.360
more information grew
larger, but luckily there

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.140
was the extra bit to work with.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.380
Things got standardized
into the Latin-1,

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.430
which uses the full 8 bits
available in the single byte.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.600
So then after that, a new
standard was developed,

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.700
UTF-8.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.510
Each character in UTF-8 is
represented using a prefix U,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.310
followed by a four-digit
hexadecimal number.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.910
The difference between binary
and hexadecimal numbers

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.685
are out of scope
for this brief talk.

00:02:34.685 --> 00:02:36.060
But what's important
to note here,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.130
though, is that UTF-8
converts a code

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.560
point or a single
character in Unicode

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.850
and to a set of 1 to 4 bytes
and can encode all 1 million

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.980
plus Unicode code points.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.250
Characters strings in R can
be encoded in Latin-1 UTF-8

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.430
or as raw bytes.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.730
These declarations
can be read or changed

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.600
using the encoding function.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.240
The concept of native
encoding is important here.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.960
Natively encoded strings are
strings written in whatever

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.040
code page the user is using.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.130
As of the 4.2.0 release, R
uses UTF-8 as the native encoding.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.580
Prior to this release,

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:11.910
that was not the case
because Windows versions

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:15.730
prior to Windows 10 didn't
allow it. Post Windows 10,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:17.730
this was allowed and R
was updated to take

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.330
advantage of this feature.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:23.310
This is an exciting change
for us focusing on NLP in R

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.590
since it mitigates a lot
of potential problems

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.310
for common NLP tasks.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.130
It's also helpful
to keep this in mind

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:33.090
when using common functions like
read.csv where character encoding

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:37.060
can be set. On Linux,
Linux, and Mac OS,

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.210
the native encoding
has been UTF-8,

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.870
so this isn't an issue there.

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:44.050
Native encoding is why we
see unknown here as well.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.170
For example, we see
coffee as unknown,

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.550
even though it's
clearly ASCII compliant.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.390
ASCII strings will never be
marked with a declared encoding

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.240
since the representation
is the same

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.780
in all supported encodings.
The sys.getlocale function

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.010
will tell you what your
native encoding is.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.400
The localization
information function

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.300
is another helpful function.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.510
And lastly, we have the
i convert function,

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.420
which uses system facilities
to convert a character

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.370
vector between encodings.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.410
So how does the tidyverse
tackle encoding?

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.380
We turn to the
stringr library, which

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:20.589
is built on top of the
extensive stringi package.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:22.650
We can specify the
encoding of a string

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.230
with the string convert
function. And for fun,

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.270
let's create a
little function here

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.570
that applies a random encoding
via the stringi string

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.050
encode a list function.

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.040
And that's it.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.570
Thanks for hanging out with me.

